---
import RuntimeLayout from "../../components/RuntimeLayout.astro";
---

<RuntimeLayout
  slug="redelivery"
  description="How watershed survives duplicate delivery: the runtime drops already-seen sequence numbers before any structure is touched, and CRDT merges are idempotent besides, so a re-delivered delta is a non-event at either layer."
>
  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>At-least-once is the honest assumption</h2>
    <p>
      Networks can deliver an update more than once. A client can resend after
      a lost acknowledgment. A <a href="/runtime/reconnect">reconnecting</a>
      client can replay data that it already received. The system must process
      repeated updates safely.
    </p>
    <p>
      The runtime checks each sequenced message first. Each message has a
      sequence number, and each client records the last sequence number that it
      applied. The runtime drops a message at or below that number before a
      structure receives it. This check protects all structure types, including
      the
      op-based <a href="/structures/counters">SharedCounter</a>, whose
      "add 5" applied twice really would be <code>+10</code>. The duplicate
      repeated operation never reaches the kernel.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>Use an idempotent merge</h2>
    <p>
      watershed's lattice counters go one layer deeper. A{" "}
      <a href="/models">CRDT</a> is built so its merge is
      <strong>idempotent</strong>: merging the same value a second time changes
      nothing. A <a href="/structures/counters#pn">PN counter</a> does not ship
      "add 5". It sends two grow-only totals. The receiver selects the larger
      value for each total. Repeating the maximum operation with the same value
      does not change the result.
    </p>
    <p>
      That puts the safety in the structure itself rather than one layer up. An
      op-based counter is safe because the runtime checks sequence numbers
      before applying; a lattice CRDT would converge even if nothing did. A
      duplicate that reached the kernel by any path (a summary overlapping a
      replay window, a sync that never passed through the sequencer) is still
      a no-op. Same outcome on the sequenced stream; a different layer holding
      the guarantee.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>Watch it absorb, live</h2>
    <p>
      The demo shows this behavior. In PN-counter mode, a
      <strong>Re-deliver last delta</strong> button re-sends the last sequenced
      delta with <em>no new sequence number</em>. It sends the delta directly to
      each replica’s kernel and skips the runtime sequence-number check. The
      merge leaves the total unchanged. The operation log records the repeated
      delivery as a muted line:
    </p>
    <figure class="oplog" aria-label="Op-log excerpt: a duplicate delta absorbed">
      <div class="oplog-line">
        <span class="oplog-sn">#03</span>
        <span class="oplog-op">cut −5 yd³ → net −5</span>
      </div>
      <div class="oplog-line oplog-dupe">
        <span class="oplog-sn">#03</span>
        <span class="oplog-op">again · cut −5 yd³ · absorbed</span>
      </div>
    </figure>
    <div class="g-note">
      <p>
        <strong>This is the line the runtime never lets an op-based counter
        see.</strong> Hand the same duplicate to <code>counter_kernel</code>
        directly and it would add again, which is why the sequence-number check
        in front of it prevents double-counting. The lattice kernels need no such guard:
        the duplicate reaches the merge and the merge shrugs. Both are safe on
        the stream; only one is safe without it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="g-out">
    <span class="annot">In short</span>
    <p>
      Every structure survives re-delivery on the sequenced stream, because the
      runtime drops any sequence number it has already seen. CRDTs carry a
      second, deeper guarantee: their merge is idempotent, so a duplicate is a
      non-event even when it reaches the kernel itself: safety that comes from
      the data type, not the delivery.
    </p>
  </div>
</RuntimeLayout>

<style>
  /* op-log excerpt — mirrors the demo's printed log: mono lines, the duplicate
     rendered as a muted italic non-event exactly as the live demo prints it. */
  .oplog {
    margin: var(--space-lg) 0 0;
    border: 1px solid var(--ink);
    background: var(--bg);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
  }

  .oplog-line {
    display: flex;
    gap: var(--space-md);
    padding: 0.5rem var(--space-lg);
  }

  .oplog-line + .oplog-line {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--hairline-faint);
  }

  .oplog-sn {
    color: var(--muted);
    min-width: 3ch;
  }

  .oplog-op {
    color: var(--ink);
  }

  .oplog-dupe .oplog-sn,
  .oplog-dupe .oplog-op {
    color: var(--muted);
    font-style: italic;
  }
</style>
